## (4) the chain, and the source lines that name each frame

The tool gives frame *attribution* (a `.pdata` range plus the records that range loads), never a
link-time name — the artifact has no symbol table. So the name comes from reading the C at the referenced
lines and taking the **enclosing definition**, which is what §45 did prose-wise for two frames and what is
done here mechanically for all five. `grep`/`Read` on `libs/quickjs/quickjs.c`, the definition line for
each frame's cited source line:

| frame rva (as printed) | `.pdata` span | offset in function | records | enclosing definition found in `libs/quickjs/quickjs.c` | name |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 `0xbd9b` | `0xbd70..0xbdf3` (131 B) | start +`0x2b` (43) | 1 | `:1492 static inline JSMallocBlockHeader *js_rc(void *ptr)`; body `:1494 return container_of(ptr, JSMallocBlockHeader, user_data);` | **`js_rc`** |
| 2 `0xeede3` | `0xeed10..0xeeff1` (737 B) | start +`0xd3` (211) | 6 | `:2355 static JSString *js_alloc_string_rt(JSRuntime *rt, int max_len, int is_wide_char)`; records at `:2361-:2366` | **`js_alloc_string_rt`** |
| 3 `0x1ebde` | `0x1ebb0..0x1eda4` (500 B) | start +`0x2e` (46) | 4 | `:3426 static JSAtom __JS_NewAtomInit(JSRuntime *rt, const char *str, int len,` (+ `:3427 int atom_type)`); records at `:3433`/`:3434` | **`__JS_NewAtomInit`** |
| 4 `0x55f5` | `0x5320..0x570b` (1003 B) | start +`0x2d5` (725) | 5 | `:3169 static int JS_InitAtoms(JSRuntime *rt)`; records at `:3174-:3178` | **`JS_InitAtoms`** |
| 5 `0x43bc` | `0x3e40..0x4f6b` (4395 B) | start +`0x57c` (1404) | 31 | `:2067 JSRuntime *JS_NewRuntime2(const JSMallocFunctions *mf, void *opaque)`; records at `:2076 … :2118` | **`JS_NewRuntime2`** |
| 6 `0x31b9c8` | `0x31ad90..0x31cbeb` (7771 B) | start +`0xc38` (3128) | 0 | no record ⇒ no source line to read; **unnamed** | (Zig side) |
| 7 `0x31cc05` | `0x31cbf0..0x31cd30` (320 B) | start +`0x15` (21) | 0 | same | (Zig side) |
| 8 `0x44e399` | `0x44e350..0x44e3cc` (124 B) | start +`0x49` (73) | 0 | same | (Zig side) |

The offsets are what a *return address* must look like, so they are recorded per frame rather than folded
into the span. All eight are inside their own span, as they must be.

So the in-DLL chain, innermost first, is:

    js_rc  (quickjs.c:1494, the UBSan trap)
      ^ called from js_alloc_string_rt  (:2355; frame 2 sits +211 into its 737-byte body)
      ^ called from __JS_NewAtomInit    (:3426; frame 3 sits +46 into its 500-byte body)
      ^ called from JS_InitAtoms        (:3169; frame 4 sits +725 into its 1003-byte body)
      ^ called from JS_NewRuntime2      (:2067; frame 5 sits +1404 into its 4395-byte body)
      ^ called from three Zig-side frames (frames 6-8, ranges above, unnamed)

**Every edge of that chain is independently present in the source**, which is what makes it a call chain
and not eight coincident ranges:

    quickjs.c:2096   if (JS_InitAtoms(rt))                          — inside JS_NewRuntime2   (5 → 4)
    quickjs.c:3191   if (__JS_NewAtomInit(rt, p, len, atom_type) …  — inside JS_InitAtoms      (4 → 3)
    quickjs.c:3430   p = js_alloc_string_rt(rt, len, 0);            — inside __JS_NewAtomInit  (3 → 2)
    quickjs.c:2361   js_rc(str)->ref_count = 1;                     — inside js_alloc_string_rt(2 → 1)

and frame 2's record list contains `:2361:17` on type `JSMallocBlockHeader` — the same header type as the
trap — so the caller of the trapping frame is carrying a check on that very call.

Three things this pins that §45 could only infer (`c2_selfabort_4_4_4.log:163-166`: "The frame between the
trap and `__JS_NewAtomInit` (RVA 0xeede3 …) has no `.pdata` entry covering it … the middle of the chain is
inferred"):

  1. **0xeede3 does have a `.pdata` entry** on the §46 bytes: `0xeed10..0xeeff1`. §45's non-coverage claim
     was about §45's bytes (`863917f2…86617`), and it is stated there as a property of the incomplete
     parse ("absence of an entry says nothing about the code"). On the bytes read here the parse covers
     the frame, so the middle of the chain is no longer inferred from call order — it is attributed by a
     range plus records plus the source line at the other end of each edge.
  2. `__JS_NewAtomInit`'s span on §46 bytes is `0x1ebb0..0x1eda4` — numerically identical to §45's
     (`c2_selfabort_4_4_4.log:101`), so the recorder that moved the outer frame did not move this one.
  3. The 131-byte trapping function carries **exactly one** UBSan record (`:1494:12`). No second site in
     that range competes for the attribution, so the "which line of this function" question has one
     answer in these bytes. (Later tasks that change the build should re-measure this, not assume it.)

## (5) interlock with §46's last record — the arena size arithmetic

§46's ring, read back from four complete renders, ends with the same event before the death
(`c2_mfrecords_4_4_4.log:274-276`, and row 3 of the table at
`evidence/analysis/runtime_444_c1_validation.md:2649-2652`):

    Mx size=ff0 ptr=1884f28 st=00000154be70

The chain above says the death sits inside the first `__JS_NewAtomInit` iteration, in `js_alloc_string_rt`
→ `js_rc`. Those are two different instruments; they agree on one number, which is the interlock. The
allocation path, read off the same source the frame names came from:

    quickjs.c:3430       p = js_alloc_string_rt(rt, len, 0);        (first iteration: len = 4, "null")
    quickjs.c:2358       js_malloc_rt(rt, sizeof(JSString) + (max_len << is_wide_char) + 1 - is_wide_char)
    quickjs.c:1800-1803  js_malloc_rt -> __js_malloc(&rt->malloc_ctx, size)
    quickjs.c:1556-1557  total_size = ((size + 7) & ~7) + sizeof(JSMallocBlockHeader)   (JS_MALLOC_ALIGN = 8, :243)
    quickjs.c:1566       block_size_idx = get_block_size_index(total_size)              (:1453-1466)
    quickjs.c:1503-1505  block_size = js_malloc_block_sizes[block_size_idx];
                         n_blocks = (JS_MALLOC_ARENA_SIZE - sizeof(JSMallocArena)) / block_size;
                         js_malloc(&s->malloc_state, sizeof(JSMallocArena) + n_blocks * block_size)

The first `JS_InitAtoms` iteration (`:3182-3193`: `p = js_atom_init`, `len = strlen(p)` at `:3190`, the
`__JS_NewAtomInit` call at `:3191`) takes the blob's first entry, `DEF(null, "null") /* must be first */`
(`libs/quickjs/quickjs-atom.h:29`), so `len = 4` and `is_wide_char = 0` — the request is
`sizeof(JSString) + 4 + 1`.

The sizes were **measured, not transcribed**. §46 got them from a throwaway probe
(`c2_mfrecords_4_4_4.log:304`); they were re-measured here the same way: a /tmp-only replica of
`struct list_head`, `JSString`, `JSMallocArena`, `JSMallocBlockHeader` typed out of `quickjs.c:583-599`,
`:289-302`, `:270-280`. The replica has no `link` field because `DUMP_LEAKS` is not defined (`:99` is commented
out and `build.zig:16-19` is the whole flag list), and the arena has no `bitmap` because `JS_MALLOC_USE_ITER`
is likewise commented out (`:257`) — either `#ifdef` would move `blocks[]` and change `sizeof(JSMallocArena)`,
so the replica is only valid for this configuration, which is the one that shipped. No repository file, no
artifact, no game involved; the probe is deliberately not in git.

    $ zig cc /tmp/layout_probe.c -o /tmp/lp && /tmp/lp          # host = x86_64-linux
    sizeof(list_head)=16
    sizeof(JSString)=12 align=4 offsetof(u)=12
    sizeof(JSMallocArena)=40 free_link@0 link@16 block_size_idx@32 n_used@34 n_blocks@36 first_free@38 blocks@40
    sizeof(JSMallocBlockHeader)=8 u@0 block_size_idx@2 ref_count@4 user_data@8
    rc=0

`blocks[]` at 40 is the `__attribute__((aligned(JS_MALLOC_ALIGN)))` of `:301` doing its job, so
`sizeof(JSMallocArena)` = 40 and the flexible array starts exactly there. The shipping DLL is
`windows`/`x86_64` (`build.zig:39-43`), and MS x86_64 gives each differently-typed bitfield its own
allocation unit, so `sizeof(JSString)` there is **20**, not 12. That is not taken on faith either — the
same numbers were asserted at the shipping target, and asserted *wrong* on each side so the check cannot
pass vacuously:

    $ zig cc -target x86_64-windows-gnu -c /tmp/layout_assert.c -o /tmp/la_win.o   # rc=0
    12 _Static_asserts pass, incl. sizeof(struct JSString) == 20 and offsetof(hash_next) == 16

    $ zig cc -c /tmp/layout_assert.c -o /tmp/la_nat.o                              # rc=1, host
    /tmp/layout_assert.c:39:16: error: static assertion failed due to requirement 'sizeof(struct JSString) == 20': jsstring_size
    /tmp/layout_assert.c:39:40: note: expression evaluates to '12 == 20'

    $ zig cc -target x86_64-windows-gnu -c /tmp/layout_assert_win12.c -o /tmp/la_win12.o   # rc=1
    /tmp/layout_assert_win12.c:12:16: error: static assertion failed due to requirement 'sizeof(struct JSString) == 12': jsstring_size_eq_12_on_windows_target
    /tmp/layout_assert_win12.c:12:40: note: expression evaluates to '20 == 12'

`JSMallocArena` = 40 and `JSMallocBlockHeader` = 8 (`user_data` @8) on **both** targets, so `JSString` is
the only layout difference in this path. Substituting per target (`JS_MALLOC_ARENA_SIZE = 4096`, `:244`):

    Windows: 20 + 4 + 1 = 25  ->  align8 32  ->  +8 header = 40  ->  idx 3, block 40
    host   : 12 + 4 + 1 = 17  ->  align8 24  ->  +8 header = 32  ->  idx 2, block 32

    $ python3 -c "print(hex(40 + ((4096-40)//40)*40), hex(40 + ((4096-40)//32)*32))"
    0xff0 0xfe8

40 + 101*40 = 4080 = `0xff0`, character-for-character the recorded size. So the arena that was handed over
successfully **immediately before the trap** is the **40-byte block class**, and the size identifies that
class uniquely among the 31 — recomputed here over `js_malloc_block_sizes` (`:1419-1451`, block_size_idx 3
= 40):

    0xff0 4080 ← only (idx 3, bs 40, n 101)
    0xfe8 4072 ← idx 2,4,5,6,7,10,12,15,18,20,23,28   (twelve classes collide here)
    0xff8 4088 ← idx 0,9,17
    0x1000 4096 ← idx 1,11

so `0xff0` is not a size two classes could produce. The block header that `js_rc` then walks backwards
from is 8 bytes (`:270-280`, `user_data` at +8, which is the `add rcx,0xfffffffffffffff8` §45 disassembled)
— the same 8 whose nullness is the trip.

The host branch is worth stating rather than skipping, because it is the arithmetic's own control group:
on the SysV layout the same string request lands in class idx 2, whose arena is **0xfe8** — and 0xfe8 is a
size §46 actually rendered, in the native positive control (`c2_mfrecords_4_4_4.log:196`,
`Mx size=fe8 ptr=2dc2e18`). The two recorded rows differ by exactly the MS-bitfield padding of `JSString`
and by nothing else in the allocator. **That is a consistency check of the layout claim, not an
identification of the native row**: the native ring is a *completed* sequence whose tail is
`Mx fe8`, `Rx 378`, `Fx 268` — not the same three allocations as the Windows renders — so reading the
native row as "the atom-name arena" would be exactly the overreach §46 warned about for its own rows 2 and
3 ("arithmetic plus call order, not an observed label", `c2_mfrecords_4_4_4.log:289-290`). Held to that
standard: the Windows `0xff0` is the interlock, the host `0xfe8` is the control.

If that arithmetic had not landed on `0xff0` the reading would have stopped there, per the brief's stop
clause. It landed.

## (6) what the chain says, and what it still does not say

The shape is now: `JS_NewRuntime2` → `JS_InitAtoms` → first `__JS_NewAtomInit` → `js_alloc_string_rt` →
`js_rc`, dying on a null `JSMallocBlockHeader` derived from a user pointer that is not NULL (§45's
"the user pointer was 8" reading, `c2_selfabort_4_4_4.log:113-116`, which this log neither re-derives nor
retracts). §43's "flat, trivial code between `:3430` and `:3330`" window and §46's "after the 40-byte
arena was delivered, before any next `mf` call" are the same place: `js_alloc_string_rt`'s body from
`:2358` (the `js_malloc_rt` that consumed the arena) to `:2361` (the first `js_rc`), with **no `mf` call in
between** — a block from the just-created arena, no callback to the host. So no additional allocator event
was expected between the `Mx size=ff0` row and the panic, and none was observed. Consistent, and worth
saying because §42 read that exact gap as 6.9 seconds of computation.

What this log does **not** establish:

  * **No function names are link-time names.** Attribution is `.pdata` range + UBSan record + the C read
    at that line, i.e. the §45 method. Nothing resolves a symbol.
  * **Frames 6-8 are unnamed and stay unnamed.** The tool printed their ranges and nothing else; naming
    them would need Zig-side attribution this task does not have (see (9)).
  * **The offsets inside each function were not decoded.** "Frame 2 = `js_alloc_string_rt` start +211" is
    arithmetic on `.pdata` bounds and the printed `ip − 1`; which instruction is at +211 and which call it
    returns from is a disassembly question, and no disassembler was run here. It belongs to the planned
    `pe_disrva` tool, not to this log.
  * **Nothing here observed why the header is null.** The mechanism is §45's open question and §46 left
    the candidate table incomplete; this log adds a location, not a cause.
  * One artifact, one digest, one host. The determinism behind it (§45's two runs, §46's 84 children with
    `distinct in-DLL RVA sets = 1`) is that of those logs, not re-measured here.

## (7) judging traps for anyone re-running this

1. **`func` wants a vma carrying the image base.** `0x18000bd9b`, not `0xbd9b`. Before the guard added in
   (8), a bare RVA fell straight through to the exit-1 branch. Reproduced here against the pre-change code
   (`git show HEAD:scripts/dll_ubsan_site.py`, run on these bytes):

       [ubsan_site] rva -0x17fffbc44 is not inside any .pdata function entry      # rc=1

   — i.e. the tool subtracted the base from a number that had none, echoed a **negative** rva, and exited 1
   with the same sentence as the genuine finding "this `.text` address is not covered by the incomplete
   `.pdata` parse" (trap 4). A negative rva in that line is already a tell, but a tell that costs nothing
   to the tool is not a fence: this is now exit 2 before anything is concluded.
   The second, nastier spelling of the same bug: bash's `$(( ))` prints **DECIMAL**, and `main` re-reads
   the argument as hex, so a base-carrying address built in bash arrives as a different number entirely.
   It is not limited to bash either — string-concatenating the base onto the rva works only when the rva is
   exactly four hex digits, and this session hit it: `0x18000` + `1ebde` = `0x180001ebde`, which is not
   `0x180000000 + 0x1ebde`, and the tool echoed `rva 0x168001ebde` for a `.text` of 0x4c9be6 bytes. The
   guard cannot catch that form (the number is above the base, so it *looks* like a vma), but the echoed rva
   is wider than the whole image, and **that is the tell**: an rva outside the image means the input number
   was wrong, not that the frame is. Build the number as `image_base + rva` in python
   (`python3 -c "print(hex(0x180000000+0x1ebde))"`) — every frame in (3) was read that way.
2. **`func`'s record lines do NOT start with the `[ubsan_site]` prefix** — they start with two spaces
   (they are `print(f"  {f}:{l}:{c} …")`). So filtering with a **literal** prefix match silently throws
   away every record line and leaves only the header and the function-range line. Measured on this
   artifact, `func 0x1800055f5` (7 output lines: 2 prefixed + 5 records):

       $ … func 0x1800055f5 | grep -F '[ubsan_site]'   → 2 lines   (the 5 records are gone)
       $ … func 0x1800055f5 | grep '\[ubsan_site\]'    → 2 lines   (same)
       $ … func 0x1800055f5 | grep '[ubsan_site]'      → 7 lines   ← note

   That third form is the trap **pointing the other way**: unescaped, `[ubsan_site]` is a grep BRE
   *character class* (any one of `u,b,s,_,a,n,i,t,e`), so it matches almost every line and filters
   nothing — a pipeline that "worked" in a previous session and dropped the record lines was using the
   escaped or `-F` form; a pipeline using the bare form looks like a filter but is a pass-through.
   Either way, do not pipe `func` through a prefix grep: pipe to `less`, or grep `quickjs\.c:`.
3. **The frame value is `ip − 1`.** Zig prints each return address one byte before the call instruction,
   so `0xbd9b` is not the faulting or calling instruction; §45 established the call as
   `0x18000bd97..0x18000bd9c` and the record load as `0x18000bd8c` (`loaded_at` above agrees). Pass the
   printed value to `func` as-is (the `.pdata` test is `begin <= rva < end`), but never read it as an
   instruction address.
4. **"Not inside any range" proves nothing.** Re-measured here on these bytes: `runtime_functions` yields
   **4,824** entries against a `.text` of vsize 0x4c9be6 — a fraction of the functions the image actually
   has, and `docs/offline-tooling.md` records **3,042** for §45's bytes (0x4c8b86). Frames 6-8 were NOT
   flagged by that trap — they hit ranges — but the inverse reading ("this frame is data or padding") is
   what §45 had to disclaim, and (4) above shows the same *kind* of address classified differently across
   two builds (0xeede3: no entry per §45, a 737-byte entry here).

5. **`this function's code references no UBSan record` is a finding, not an error.** It exits 1 and it
   means: this function's bytes contain no `lea` of any record the scanner knows. For Zig-side code that
   is expected (Zig's checks do not carry a `TypeMismatchData` record with a C file/line), which is exactly
   why frames 6-8 came back this way. It does NOT mean the function is unreachable or misattributed.
6. **`rc=3` is still not a step code.** Nothing in this log re-endorses reading the process exit status as
   a position in the init sequence (§38.5, §45.4).

## (8) the guard this job installed, and the exit-code contract

A bare RVA was fed to `func` twice in the session that produced this log: once by accident (the
concatenation slip of trap 1, answered by the exit-1 "not inside any `.pdata` function entry" sentence),
and once on purpose against `git show HEAD:scripts/dll_ubsan_site.py` to record what the tool did **before**
the change. A wrong number in the caller's own hand must not come out looking like a conclusion about the
image, so `cmd_func` now checks the caliber before subtracting the base (`scripts/dll_ubsan_site.py`), and
prints one line on **stderr**, the stream the tool's other caller-side errors already use:

    $ python3 scripts/dll_ubsan_site.py zig-out/bin/stellaris_quickjs.dll func 0x43bc
    [ubsan_site] zig-out/bin/stellaris_quickjs.dll image_base=0x180000000 mode=func      # stdout
    [ubsan_site] func takes a vma carrying the image base (0x180000000); 0x43bc is below it, so it looks like a bare rva — pass 0x1800043bc for rva 0x43bc   # stderr
    rc=2

The contract, and the two exit-1 messages stay exactly as they were and keep **different meanings** — a
later reader must not collapse them:

| code | meaning | where it is printed |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | the artifact was read and something matched | `site` with rows; `func` with a covering range **and** ≥1 referenced record |
| 1 | the artifact was read and **nothing** matched — a finding | `site`: no check site for that type name. `func`: **either** "rva `X` is not inside any `.pdata` function entry" (no covering range — uninformative, trap 4) **or** "this function's code references no UBSan record" (a range was found, no record inside it — the normal answer for Zig code, trap 5) |
| 2 | the invocation was wrong, so nothing was concluded about the artifact | cannot read the path / truncated / not PE32+; **or** `func` was handed a number below the image base |

Code 2's name in the module changed from `EXIT_READ_ERROR` to `EXIT_CALLER_ERROR` (the constant's old name
covered only the I/O half; no evidence log cites the constant, and the frozen `scratch/2026-10_abortexit/`
original keeps its own copy untouched). The guard is pinned by one new test function in
`scripts/test_c2_harness.py::test_ubsan_site_func_rejects_a_bare_rva_instead_of_guessing`, which asserts
both sides (bare rva → 2 + "image base" on stderr and no `.pdata` sentence anywhere in the output;
`image_base + rva` → 0) against an address **derived from the image**, not hard-coded, for the reason the
sibling `func` test states. Fence counts as actually printed after the change:

    $ python3 -m pytest scripts/test_scripts_layout.py -q
    23 passed in 0.12s
    $ python3 -m pytest scripts/test_c2_harness.py -q
    134 passed in 1.09s                    (was 133 before this job's one added test)
    $ PYTHONPATH=scripts/windows python3 -m pytest scripts/windows/tests -q
    169 passed in 4.62s

Nothing in `src/`, `build.zig`, or `libs/quickjs/` was touched, and the artifact re-checked at commit time:

    $ sha256sum zig-out/bin/stellaris_quickjs.dll
    77c0c35ec5b74abc8ccb849515621aaf53abd3fdc0a5e6ab8b9466acc0c11ad0  zig-out/bin/stellaris_quickjs.dll

